<description>
Assemble colored sprites into a central bar's notches, chaining successive pieces along fixed-width strips.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. Largest monochrome component is a solid "bar" with boundary notches (bg holes opening to its bbox edge).
- Several smaller colored sprites scattered around the bar.

Rule:
- Each bar notch is filled by a root piece whose shape exactly covers the hole.
- The root defines a strip (column-span or row-span) for its chain.
- Successive pieces fill the bg cells inside the previous piece's bbox, locked to the same strip width.
- Multiple chains grow independently from different bar notches.

Key insight: The root piece's extent perpendicular to the chain direction locks the strip width for all subsequent pieces in that chain.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_to_match",
  "src": "dangling monochrome sprite s",
  "dst": "plug into central bar or previously plugged sprite d",
  "match_cond": "s.shape fits into d.notch.shape",
  "multi_step": "place sprites one into the notch of another until no notches remain; multiple chains grow from different bar notches"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 30x30; output same shape as input
  bg: 0 (black)
  bar:
    orientation: horizontal or vertical
    span: full grid width (horizontal) or full grid height (vertical)
    thickness: 5-7 cells perpendicular to span
    color: single non-bg color; largest monochromatic 4-connected component
    position: center_frac 0.35-0.53 along perpendicular axis
    notches: 6-9 holes opening to the bar bbox boundary (perpendicular sides)
    notch_areas: 1-10 cells each; depth 1-2 cells into bar
    used_notches: 1-2 notches filled by root pieces (= number of chains)
    decoy_notches: 4-7 unfilled notches that add ambiguity
    no_internal_holes: all empty cells within bar bbox touch its boundary
  chains: |
    1-2 independent chains grow outward from distinct used notches.
    Each chain has 2-3 monochromatic pieces sharing a fixed strip width (4-5).
    Strip axis = bar span axis (cols for horizontal bar, rows for vertical).
    Root piece plugs into notch (its cells cover all notch cells + extend outside bar).
    Each successive piece exactly fills the bg cavity within the previous piece's
    bbox (restricted to the strip), plus extends further from the bar.
    The tail piece has zero cavity (chain terminates).
  pieces:
    count: 3-6 total across all chains
    area: 8-13 cells each
    bbox_fill: 0.54-0.80
    strip_dim: all pieces in a chain share the same perpendicular extent (= strip width)
    chain_dim: 2-6 cells along the chain growth direction; varies per piece
    colors: each piece is a single non-bg, non-bar color; mostly distinct [observed]
    connectivity: 4-connected
    no_rotation: pieces keep original orientation (no flip/rotate)
  input_layout: |
    Pieces are scattered around the bar (not touching bar or each other).
    Distance from bar: 3-8 cells. Pieces placed on either side of the bar.

generation_strategy: |
  Output-first approach:
  1. Pick grid 30x30, bg=0, bar color, orientation, thickness (5-7), position.
  2. Build bar spanning full grid dimension. Carve 6-9 notch holes on perpendicular
     edges (depth 1-2, area 1-10). Designate 1-2 as "used" notches.
  3. For each used notch, build a chain of 2-3 pieces outward from bar:
     a. Root piece: shape covers the notch cells + extends outside bar bbox with
        total strip width 4-5. Ensure fill ratio 0.54-0.80.
     b. Next piece: shape must exactly cover root's bg cavity (within strip) and
        extend further. Same strip width, different color.
     c. If chain length=3, repeat for third piece. Tail piece must have zero cavity.
  4. Assemble output: bar + all pieces placed in their chain positions.
  5. Derive input: keep bar unchanged, relocate each piece to a random scattered
     position 3-8 cells from bar, ensuring no overlap with bar or other pieces.
  6. Validate: run solver on input and confirm it matches the assembled output.
```
